<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">André Grétry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1741">1741</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as André-Ernest-Modeste Grétry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">André-Ernest-Modeste Grétry was a composer from Liège who made his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and became the leading composer of French opéra comique in the late eighteenth century. His works included Zémire et Azor and Richard Cœur-de-lion (1784), and he was honoured by both the old monarchy and the Revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andre-gretry.jpg" alt="André Grétry" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Élisabeth Louise Vigée Le Brun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andr%C3%A9_Ernest_Modeste_Gr%C3%A9try.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>André Ernest Modeste Grétry was baptised on 11 February 1741 in Liège, then part of the Prince-Bishopric of Liège, the son of a poor musician. He was a choirboy at the church of St. Denis and from 1753 studied with Jean-Pantaléon Leclerc and later with Nicolas Rennekin. Of greater importance was the practical lesson of attending performances by an Italian opera company, which made him wish to complete his studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He composed a mass in 1759, dedicated to the canons of Liège Cathedral, and Canon Hurley paid for his journey. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he spent five years under Giovanni Battista Casali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first success was the Italian intermezzo La vendemmiatrice, composed for a Roman theatre. After studying a score by Pierre-Alexandre Monsigny he decided to devote himself to French comic opera, and he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on New Year's Day 1767. He stopped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, and then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. After two years of poverty and obscurity, the Swedish ambassador, Count Gustaf Philip Creutz, obtained a libretto for him from Jean-François Marmontel. As Le Huron it was performed in August 1768 with great success.</p><p>He composed some fifty operas. His masterpieces were Zémire et Azor of 1771 and Richard Coeur-de-lion of 1784. A romance from the latter, O Richard, O mon Roi, was sung at the banquet given by the bodyguard to the officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> garrison on 3 October 1789. He was the first to write for the tuba curva, which he used in music for the funeral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, and his opera-ballet La caravane du Caire, first performed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> in 1783, stayed in the French repertory for fifty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Grétry lost much of his property in the Revolution, but successive French governments favoured him. The old court gave him distinctions and rewards, the republic made him an inspector of the conservatoire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> granted him the cross of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour and a pension. Mozart and Beethoven were influenced by his music, and both wrote variations on his works.</p><p>He died at the Hermitage in Montmorency, formerly the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>. Fifteen years later his heart was moved, after a protracted lawsuit, to Liège, where a bronze statue of him was set up in 1842 and still holds it. His body is buried at Père Lachaise Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “André Grétry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The composer Johannes Ciconia, born in Liège in the fourteenth century, and the eighteenth-century opera composer <b>André-Ernest-Modeste Grétry</b> carried Liège music abroad, and in 1756 Pierre Rousseau founded the Journal encyclopédique in Liège, an important organ of the French Enlightenment, which later moved to Bouillon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rue des Récollets, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Montmorency, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.64,5.58&amp;year=1787" aria-label="Rue des Récollets on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of André Grétry"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5302 5784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5302 5784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.64,5.58&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
